(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027-068"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027-068","title":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Discrete Mathematics | Topic Wise Test 3","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Discrete Mathematics | Topic Wise Test 3","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027","num":68,"category":"topicwise","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2700,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Discrete Mathematics"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027/107_exam_1318_GO Classes CS Test Series Discrete Mathematics Topic Wise Test 3.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"What is the order of smallest non-cyclic group?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 15)</small></h4><a name=\"376102\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=6613&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">Smallest Non-ableian group, Smallest Non-cyclic group</a>&nbsp;(<strong>Watch Full Video</strong> if you haven't)</p><p>For order $4,$ we have two non-isomorphic groups. One of these is Cyclic Group, another isn’t.<br>The template where we have an identity element, one element who is inverse of itself, and two elements who are inverse of each other. It is a cyclic group.<br><br>The template where we have an identity element, and every element is inverse of itself. It is a &nbsp;group But not cyclic.</p><hr><p><strong>Group Theory Complete Summary, GATE PYQs, Practice Questions Playlist:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgsZOgtGQ6Yg_KR7sedZOgT&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgsZOgtGQ6Yg_KR7sedZOgT&amp;feature=shared</a></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"469072\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Order 4 group has two non isomorphic structures. <br>\n<br>\nTemplate -1 :<br>\n<br>\n(e,x,y,z) e^-1=e , x-1=x , y^-1 =z &nbsp;This structure is abelian, cyclic and is a group. the element other than identity element can generate the whole group.<br>\n<br>\nTemplate -2 :<br>\n<br>\n(e,x,y,z) e^-1=e , x-1=x , y^-1 =y &nbsp;z^-1 =z. This structure is a abelian group but not cyclic because no generator is there. <br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376101\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":2,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Let $\\text{G}$ be a cyclic group with generator ‘$a$’. Order of ‘$a$’ is $29.$ The number of subgroups $\\text{G}$ has ________","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 27)</small></h4><a name=\"376152\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Order of $’a’$ is $29$. Also, $a$ is generator of $G$ i.e., $&lt;a&gt;=G$.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>So, Order of $G=29$.</p>\n\n<p>Any subgroup of $G$ must have an order that divides 29 (<em>By Lagrange’s Theorem</em>).</p>\n\n<p>Only, $1$ and $29$ divide $29$. So, <strong>only two subgroups are possible</strong>. Those are the trivial Subgroups itself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> For any Group of prime order there are only two subgroups.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"376100\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Since “$a$” is a generator, the order of “$a$” is the same as the order of the group.<br>Order of generators is the same as Order of the group.<br>So, the group $\\text{G}$ has order $29,$ which is a prime number, hence only two subgroups are possible.</p><p><strong>Order of an element in a group:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=4113&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=4113&amp;feature=shared</a></p><p><strong>Lagrange's Theorem in group theory:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=10564&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=10564&amp;feature=shared</a></p><hr><p><strong>Group Theory Complete Summary, GATE PYQs, Practice Questions Playlist:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgsZOgtGQ6Yg_KR7sedZOgT&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgsZOgtGQ6Yg_KR7sedZOgT&amp;feature=shared</a></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"408068\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Improper subgroups are also sub groups that’s why &lt;e&gt; and {G} itself these 2 are subgroups here.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376099\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>In a group $\\text{G},$ every element other than the identity element has order $2$ then $\\mathrm{G}$ is?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Abelian group</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Cyclic group</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Non-abelian group</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Non-cyclic group</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 20)</small></h4><a name=\"376098\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Explanation, with Proof &amp; Variations:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s</a></p><p>If $a^{2}=e$ for all elements $a$ in a group $G$, then $G$ must be abelian.<br><br>If every element other than $e$ had order $2,$ it means that for all elements $a, a^{2}=e$, hence, every element is inverse of itself.<br><br>Since every element is inverse of itself, hence, $G$ is abelian group.</p><hr><p>In a group, if $a^2 = e$, then $a = a^{-1}.$</p><p>Proof:</p><p>$a^2 = e$ ; So $a.a = e$ ; Multiply both sides with $a^{-1}$ on LHS, we get $a^{-1}.a.a = a^{-1}.e$, which becomes $e.a = a^{-1}$, which becomes $a = a^{-1}.$<br><br><strong>Detailed Video Explanation, with Proof &amp; Variations:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s</a></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"468026\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Explanation, with Proof &amp; Variations:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s</a></p><p>“If for all $x \\in \\text{G} , x^{2} = 1,$ then $\\text{G}$ is commutative. Here, $1$ is the identity element of $\\text{G}.$” : True<br><br>$\\textbf{Proof 1 :}$<br>Let $a,b$ be two elements in group $\\text{G}.$ Consider the element $ab.$<br>Now, $(ab)^{2} = 1$<br>$ab.ab = 1$<br>Now, multiply both sides on the right with $b,$<br>$aba=b$<br>Now, multiply both sides on the right with $a,$<br>$ab=ba$<br>Hence, $\\text{G}$ is abelian.<br><br>$\\textbf{Proof 2:}$<br>Let $a,b$ be two elements in group $\\text{G}.$<br>It is given that $(ab)^{2}=1 ;$ So, since $(ab)(ab)=1,$ So, $ab$ is inverse of $ab.$<br>Now consider $(ab)(ba).$<br>$(ab)(ba)=a(bb)a=aa=1 ;$ So, $(ab)(ba)=1 ;$ which means that $ba$ is inverse of $ab.$<br>But every element in a group has unique inverse, So, $ab=ba.$<br>Hence, $\\text{G}$ is abelian.<br>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Detailed Video Explanation, with Proof &amp; Variations:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27dpP0LS00Q&amp;t=517s</a></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"404916\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The actual proof for G is abelian is given above but we can eliminate options one by one :</p>\n\n<p><strong>option A : abelian</strong></p>\n\n<p>Yes, may be. $U_{8}$ is abelian&nbsp;, but needs proof.</p>\n\n<p><strong>option B : cyclic</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>here&nbsp;$U_{8}$ is a counter eg as it is not cyclic ! Hence, false.</p>\n\n<p><strong>option C : non-abelian</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>here also&nbsp;$U_{8}$ is counter eg as it is abelian ! Hence, false.</p>\n\n<p><strong>option D : non-cyclic</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>here&nbsp;$U_{8}$ won’t be sufficient to prove entire statement.</p>\n\n<p>consider a group of order 2 : ({e, a}, *)</p>\n\n<p>$a = a^{-1}$</p>\n\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:98px\">\n\t<tbody>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:16px\">&nbsp; <strong>*</strong></td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:37px\"><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; e</strong></td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:29px\"><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp;a</strong></td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:16px\">&nbsp;<strong>e</strong></td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:37px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; e</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:29px\">&nbsp; &nbsp;a</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:16px\">&nbsp;<strong>a</strong></td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:37px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; a</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:29px\">&nbsp; &nbsp;e</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>&nbsp;a is generator since &lt;a&gt; = {e, a}</p>\n\n<p>Thus, Cyclic ! Hence, false !</p>\n\n<p>also note that order-2 group is a prime order group with&nbsp;$a^{2} = e$ &amp; every prime order group is cyclic!</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"469073\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">The language was confusing me so what i thought of this is like given below:<br>\n<br>\nwe know that if the order of an element is given by a^n=e where n belongs to integers(Z)<br>\n<br>\nHere it is given order of element is 2 so we can conclude by the defination of order of element <br>\n<br>\nSo by defination, <br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;a^2 =e <br>\n<br>\nnow from here we know that a^-1 = a if we multiply both sides by a^-1.<br>\n<br>\nTo prove abelian , ab = ba<br>\n<br>\n(ab)^-1 = b^-1 a^-1 - (i)<br>\n<br>\nand we know (ab)^-1 = ab , b^-1=b and a^-1 = a<br>\n<br>\nputting above in (i)<br>\n<br>\nab = ba . So by this we can say if the order of every element other then identity element is 2 then the group is abelian.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"531427\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p dir=\"ltr\"><b id=\"docs-internal-guid-1dde00dd-7fff-5087-6b2a-ac410fade952\">In any group :(ab)^−1=b^−1a^-1</b></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b>&nbsp;given in question a^2=e so a=a^-1 every element is inverse of itself so</b></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b>from this&nbsp;</b><b>(ab)^−1=b^−1a^-1 we can say that&nbsp;</b></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b>ab=ba(because (ab)^-1=ab(treat ab as single element),b^-1=b and a^-1=a</b></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376097\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":4,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"What is the smallest positive integer $n$ such that there is a group of order $n$ which is not abelian?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"376311\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Okay let proceed with the answer, please note the following points:</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:decimal\" type=\"1\">\n\t<li>There is only one non isomorphic group of order 1 i.e ({e}, *) and this is Cyclic.\n\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:63px; border-spacing: 1px;\">\n\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:21px\">*</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:27px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:21px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:27px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t</tbody>\n\t</table>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>For a prime number p, every group of order p is cyclic. So group with order 2, 3, 5 are cyclic.<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>For Order 4, only 2&nbsp;non isomorphic groups are there which are:&nbsp;<br>\n\t<br>\n\tTemplate 1:\n\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:155px; border-spacing: 1px;\">\n\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">*</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:24px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:23px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t</tbody>\n\t</table>\n\t<br>\n\tTemplate 2:\n\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:155px; border-spacing: 1px;\">\n\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">*</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:24px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:23px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"height:11px; width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:24px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">c</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">b</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:23px\">e</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:26px\">a</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t</tbody>\n\t</table>\n\t<br>\n\tAs you can see both are Abelian.<br>\n\t<br>\n\tOR<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>Groups of order&nbsp;$p^{2}$, where p&nbsp;is&nbsp;prime, are necessarily Abelian. That rules out&nbsp;4&nbsp;as possible order of&nbsp;non-Abelian&nbsp;groups.<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>There are&nbsp;two&nbsp;groups of order&nbsp;6. One of these is&nbsp;cyclic. The other is the symmetric group on&nbsp;3&nbsp;symbols,&nbsp;S3.&nbsp;This group is non-Abelian.<br>\n\t<br>\n\tExample:<br>\n\t&nbsp;\n\t<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:360px\">\n\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<th>Element</th>\n\t\t\t\t<th style=\"width: 49px;\">123</th>\n\t\t\t\t<th style=\"width: 41px;\">213</th>\n\t\t\t\t<th style=\"width: 42px;\">132</th>\n\t\t\t\t<th style=\"width: 43px;\">321</th>\n\t\t\t\t<th style=\"width: 39px;\">231</th>\n\t\t\t\t<th style=\"width: 45px;\">312</th>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td><strong>123</strong></td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:49px\">123</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:41px\">213</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:42px\">132</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:43px\">321</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:39px\">231</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:45px\">312</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td><strong>213</strong></td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:49px\">213</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:41px\">123</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:42px\">231</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:43px\">312</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:39px\">132</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:45px\">321</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td><strong>132</strong></td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:49px\">132</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:41px\">312</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:42px\">123</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:43px\">231</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:39px\">321</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:45px\">213</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td><strong>321</strong></td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:49px\">321</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:41px\">231</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:42px\">312</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:43px\">123</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:39px\">213</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:45px\">132</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td><strong>231</strong></td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:49px\">231</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:41px\">321</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:42px\">213</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:43px\">132</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:39px\">312</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:45px\">123</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t\t<td><strong>312</strong></td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:49px\">312</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:41px\">132</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:42px\">321</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:43px\">213</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:39px\">123</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td style=\"width:45px\">231</td>\n\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t</tbody>\n\t</table>\n\t<br>\n\tReference:&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://groupprops.subwiki.org/wiki/Symmetric_group:S3\" target=\"_blank\">https://groupprops.subwiki.org/wiki/Symmetric_group:S3</a><br>\n\t<br>\n\t<strong>So, Answer is 6.</strong></li>\n</ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 13)</small></h4><a name=\"374610\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Groups of Small Orders:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=231&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=231&amp;feature=shared</a></p><p><strong>Some Important Points about Abelian Groups:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=2180&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=2180&amp;feature=shared</a></p><p>The smallest non-abelian group has order $6.$<br><br>$\\textbf{(Lagrange’s theorem) :}$ &nbsp;Let $\\text{G}$ be a finite group and $\\text{H} \\subset &nbsp;\\text{G}$ a subgroup of $\\text{G}.$ Then $|\\text{H}|$ divides $|\\text{G}|.$<br><br>We know that If $\\text{G}$ is a finite group with $|\\text{G}|$ prime, then $\\text{G}$ is cyclic.<br><br>$\\textbf{Proof :}$ Let $p = |\\text{G}|.$ Since $p \\geq 2,$ there is an element $g \\in \\text{G}$ with $g \\neq e.$ Consider the subgroup $\\langle g \\rangle \\subset G$ generated by $g.$ We have $| \\langle g \\rangle | \\geq 2$ since both $e, g &nbsp;\\in \\langle g \\rangle.$ So by Lagrange’s theorem $| \\langle g \\rangle | = p.$ Thus $\\langle g \\rangle = \\text{G},$ and so, by definition, $\\text{G}$ is cyclic as claimed.<br><br>Every cyclic group is abelian.<br><br>$\\text{Classification of Groups of Order}\\; n \\leq 5 :$</p><ul><li>$n=1:$ The trivial group $\\langle e \\rangle$ is the only group with $1$ element.</li><li>$n=2,3,5 :$ These orders are prime, so Lagrange implies that any such group is cyclic.</li><li>$n=4 :$ There are only two groups of order $4,$ both are abelian.</li></ul><p><strong>Groups of Small Orders:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=231&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=231&amp;feature=shared</a></p><p><strong>Some Important Points about Abelian Groups:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=2180&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/live/Zb4OWjHL3yU?t=2180&amp;feature=shared</a></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374609\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbf{Z}$ be the group of all integers under the operation of addition. Which of the following subsets of $\\mathbf{Z}$ is/are NOT a subgroup of $\\mathbf{Z}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\{0\\}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\{n \\in \\mathbf{Z} : n &gt; 0\\}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\{n \\in \\mathbf{Z} : n\\;\\text{is an even integer}\\}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\{n \\in \\mathbf{Z} : n \\;\\text{is divisible by both 6 and 9}\\}$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"377964\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Since the operation is addition and addition is associative and a binary operation and its closed, Z under addition is a <strong>semigroup</strong></p>\n\n<p>For <strong>monoid</strong>, it&nbsp;must satisfy the properties of a semigroup and an unique identity must exist. For Z under addition, 0 is the identity element and&nbsp;$\\forall x \\epsilon Z, x + 0 = x$ and hence Z under addition is a monoid</p>\n\n<p>A <strong>Group </strong>must satisfy the properties of a semigroup , monoid and in addition to that has an inverse element and also its closed under all operations</p>\n\n<p>Given a group G, subset H of G is a <strong>subgroup</strong> of G if H also forms a group under the same operation</p>\n\n<p>Order of the group is number of elements in the group (or) its cardinality</p>\n\n<p>Also, by <strong>Lagrange’s theorem</strong>, order of the subgroup divides the order of the group</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\" type=\"A\">\n\t<li>Has only one element 0.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For addition operation, 0 is the identity and 0 + inverse(0) = identity $\\Rightarrow$ 0 + 0 = 0. As we can see this subset is a subgroup as it satisfies properties of a group and also its closed</p>\n\n<ol start=\"2\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\" type=\"A\">\n\t<li>Identity element 0 is not part of the subset.&nbsp;&nbsp;Inverse for each element is also not part of the subset. Since this subset is not closed , this is not a subgroup<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>0 is an even integer and part of the subset. For every even integer say x, -x is the inverse and -x is also part of the subset. Since this subset is closed , identity &amp; inverse exists, this is a subgroup<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>n is divisible by 6 and 9&nbsp;$\\Rightarrow$ n is divisible by LCM(6,9) which is 18. So, this subset contains multiples of 18 such as …, -54, -36, -18, 0, 18, 36, ….<br>\n\tIdentity element is 0 which is part of the subset<br>\n\tInverse of each element x is -x which is also unique and part of the subset.&nbsp;<br>\n\tHence, this subset is a subgroup<br>\n\t<br>\n\t<strong>Hence, correct option&nbsp;is B</strong></li>\n</ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"409239\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">By intuition<br>\n<br>\nIf H is a subgroup of group G, H must include group G's identity element<br>\n<br>\nOther than e , if a є G then their power of an element must be included in the subgroup<br>\n<br>\nA. It is a subgroup <br>\n<br>\nC. All even integers {.......-4,-2,0,2,4......}<br>\n<br>\nD. {........-18,0,18,........}<br>\n<br>\nB. Identity element itself not present since n&gt;0. So, it is not a subgroup</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"404919\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>subgroups of Z : {0} &amp; nZ for every integer n</p>\n\n<p><strong>option A :</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>{0} –&nbsp;the only finite subgroup of Z , rest all sub-groups are nZ for every integer n</p>\n\n<p><strong>option B :</strong></p>\n\n<p>{n | n &gt; 0} = {1, 2, 3, ...} , it is not even a group (no identity element!)</p>\n\n<p><strong>option C :</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>it is actually 2Z = {...-2, -1, 0, 1, 2,...}</p>\n\n<p><strong>option D :</strong></p>\n\n<p>it is actually subgroup 18Z&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"456048\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>For Subgroups, we need to check 3 properties :</p><ol><li>Closure</li><li>Identity</li><li>Inverse</li></ol><p><em>A. </em>{0} : Trivially closed,&nbsp;0 is the identity, 0 is the inverse of itself<br>&nbsp;</p><p><em>B. Let (G2,+) = ({1,2,3,.....} , +)&nbsp;</em></p><ul><li>G2 is closed as sum of any numbers from the set will be positive integer</li><li>Let's assume identity&nbsp;= e<br>2 + e = 2&nbsp;<br>e = 0 (does not belong to the set) Therefore, identity does not exist<br><strong>G2 is not a Subgroup of Z</strong><br>&nbsp;</li></ul><div><i>C.&nbsp;</i><em>Let (G3,+) = ({-8,-6,-4,-2,0,2,4,6,8,.....} , +)&nbsp;</em></div><ul><li>G3&nbsp;is closed as sum of any numbers from the set will result in even&nbsp;integer</li><li>Identity = 0</li><li>Inverse : Negative counterparts of the elements<br>Thus, G3 is also a Subgroup<br>&nbsp;</li></ul><p><i>D.&nbsp;</i><em>Let (G4,+) = ({-72,-54,-36,-18,0,18,36,54,72,.....} , +)&nbsp;</em></p><ul><li>Similarly, G4 is also closed, identity = 0 and inverse :&nbsp;Negative counterparts of the elements . Thus G4 is also a Subgroup<br>&nbsp;</li></ul><p><strong>Thus, Answer = B</strong></p><p><br><br>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"506501\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,0,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Question:</b> Which subset of integers is NOT a subgroup under addition?<button aria-label=\"View source details. Opens side panel.\" data-hveid=\"0\" decode-data-ved=\"1\" data-ved=\"0CAAQvoAQahgKEwjhgpKK09uRAxUAAAAAHQAAAAAQwAU\"><br></button><button aria-label=\"View source details. Opens side panel.\" data-hveid=\"0\" decode-data-ved=\"1\" data-ved=\"0CAAQvoAQahgKEwjhgpKK09uRAxUAAAAAHQAAAAAQwQU\"></button></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Logic:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,0,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">A:</b> $\\{0\\}$ is the trivial subgroup.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,1,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">B:</b> $\\{n \\in Z : n &gt; 0\\}$ (Positive integers) fails because it lacks the identity (0) and inverse elements (negatives).&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,2,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">C:</b> Even integers form the subgroup $2Z$.&nbsp;<button aria-label=\"View source details. Opens side panel.\" data-hveid=\"0\" decode-data-ved=\"1\" data-ved=\"0CAAQvoAQahgKEwjhgpKK09uRAxUAAAAAHQAAAAAQxAU\"></button></p></li></ul></li></ul><div>&nbsp;</div><ul><li><ul data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,3,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,1,3,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">D:</b> Integers divisible by both 6 and 9 are multiples of $LCM(6, 9) = 18$. $18Z$ is a subgroup.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></li></ul></li></ul><div>&nbsp;</div><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,2,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Correct Answer:</b> <b data-path-to-node=\"11,2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"16\">B</b></p></li></ul></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374599\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The multiplication table $\\left({ }^*\\right)$ for a group $\\mathrm{G}=\\{\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{d})$ is given below.<br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}<br>\\hline \\ast &amp; a &amp; b &amp; c &amp; d \\\\<br>\\hline a &amp; a &amp; b &amp; c &amp; d \\\\<br>\\hline b &amp; b &amp; a &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\\hline c &amp; c &amp; &amp; a &amp; \\\\<br>\\hline d &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; a \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\end{array}$$<br>Fill in the rest of the table.</p><p>Which of the following is/are false?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$d \\ast b=d$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$b \\ast c=d$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\left(c\\ast d\\right) \\ast c=d$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$G$ is a cyclic group.</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"403582\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Since $G$ is a group, we get the following table:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{c|cccc}<br>\n\\ast &amp; \\mathrm{a} &amp; \\mathrm{b} &amp; \\mathrm{c} &amp; \\mathrm{d} \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\mathrm{a} &amp; \\mathrm{a} &amp; \\mathrm{b} &amp; \\mathrm{c} &amp; \\mathrm{d} \\\\<br>\n\\mathrm{b} &amp; \\mathrm{b} &amp; \\mathrm{a} &amp; \\mathrm{d} &amp; \\mathrm{c} \\\\<br>\n\\mathrm{c} &amp; \\mathrm{c} &amp; \\mathrm{d} &amp; \\mathrm{a} &amp; \\mathrm{b} \\\\<br>\n\\mathrm{d} &amp; \\mathrm{d} &amp; \\mathrm{c} &amp; \\mathrm{b} &amp; \\mathrm{a}<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 11)</small></h4><a name=\"435730\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c09b7607b3df61ab7ece.jpg\">a,d&nbsp; are false&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"438388\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer A,D.<br><br>From table given we can see that every element is inverse of its own which means the order of every element is 2. For a group to be cyclic at least one element should have an equal order of group.<br>&nbsp;</p><hr><ul><li><h3><strong>Theorem</strong> : If every element is inverse of its own then it is an abelian group.</h3></li></ul><hr><p><br>Now you can quickly fill table and can find answer.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/403581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{R}$ be the set of all real numbers.</p><p>Let $\\text{S = R} \\backslash\\{-1\\}$ and define a binary operation on $\\text{S}$ by $a \\ast b=a+b+a b$.</p><p>Which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$(\\mathrm{S}, \\ast)$ is a not a group.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$(\\mathrm{S}, \\ast)$ is a group but not abelian.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$(\\mathrm{S}, \\ast)$ is a cyclic group.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$(\\mathrm{S}, \\ast)$ is an abelian group but not cyclic.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 30)</small></h4><a name=\"376090\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"244\" src=\"media/5a/5ab63214ce4b325446cb.png\" width=\"705\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"259\" src=\"media/69/69f29a9ef529aaa524c7.png\" width=\"709\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"292\" src=\"media/02/023b0f780c93849e1520.png\" width=\"697\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"128\" src=\"media/e1/e1e48aa742dc96111ef4.png\" width=\"694\"></strong><br>\n<br>\nNote that $(\\mathrm{S}, *)$ is not a cyclic group because there is NO element which can generate all the real numbers.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376089\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":8,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following&nbsp;multiplication tables defined on the set $G = \\{a, b, c, d\\}$ form a group?<br>&nbsp;</p><p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"275\" width=\"707\" src=\"media/a9/a927de719b8484f5912f.png\"></strong></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Option A</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Option B</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Option C</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Option D</p>"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"376088\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Option A table doesn’t have any identity element. There is no row exactly identical to the header. So, It’s not a group since it doesn’t have an identity. The easy way to see that it does not have an identity is that no row of the Cayley table matches the top row.<br>\n<br>\nOption C table forms a group. It is the standard template where we have an identity element, one element who is inverse of itself, and two elements who are inverse of each other. So, it is a cyclic group.<br>\nThe identity is a. All elements have inverses (the inverse of a is a, the inverse of b is b, the inverse of c is c and the inverse of d is d).<br>\n<br>\n<br>\nOption B table forms a group. It is the standard template where we have an identity element, and every element is inverse of itself. So, it is a &nbsp;group But not cyclic. The identity is a. All elements have inverses (the inverse of a is a, the inverse of b is b, the inverse of c is c and the inverse of d is d).<br>\n<br>\n<br>\nOption D is Not a group because, in the last row, element “d” repeats.<br>\nThe identity is a. However, d does not have an inverse, so it is not a group.<br>\n<br>\nAlso, we know that every group of orders $4$ is abelian, hence we can eliminate options D, and A, as those are not commutative.<br>\n<br>\n<br>\nAnalysis of Option C:<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"86\" src=\"media/bd/bdf106107358097bc971.png\" width=\"704\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"296\" src=\"media/00/00d61b4a1cb42529210c.png\" width=\"736\"></strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"378296\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Ans is Option B and C.</strong></p>\n\n<p>An easy approach in these type of questions:-</p>\n\n<p>First check which row is exactly same as the header row (a,b,c,d). The corresponding element in the operation column is the identity element.</p>\n\n<p>Here,&nbsp;in&nbsp;<strong>option A,&nbsp;</strong>there is no such row. Hence no identity element. Hence&nbsp;<strong>option A is wrong.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Now in options B,C,D there is such a row, and all of them have the identity element as&nbsp;<strong>$’a’$.&nbsp;</strong>Now identity element must be in every row. But its absent in&nbsp;<strong>option D. So option D is wrong.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Now if we check in&nbsp;<strong>option B and C,&nbsp;</strong>we can see that they satisfy all the conditions. And every element has their own inverses. Inverses can be found by intersecting the corresponding row element and column&nbsp; element where the identity element (here $a$) is present.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Eg: In&nbsp;<strong>Option B →&nbsp;</strong>Inverse of a is a, b is b, c is c and d is d.</p>\n\n<p>And in <strong>Option C&nbsp;</strong><strong>→&nbsp;</strong>Inverse of a is a, b is d, c is c and d is b.<strong>&nbsp;</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376087\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $G$ be a group under binary operation $\\ast.$ Let $g \\in G$.</p><p>We define $\\langle g\\rangle$ as follows :<br>$$\\langle g\\rangle=\\left\\{g^{n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}$$</p><p>Which of the following is /are true about $\\langle g\\rangle$ under binary operation $\\ast ?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\langle g\\rangle$ is also a group.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\langle g\\rangle$ is a cyclic subgroup of $G$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\langle g\\rangle$ is abelian.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $H \\leq G$ and $g \\in H$, then $\\langle  g\\rangle \\leq H$.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"376074\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>This is a very basic and standard result in group theory.<br>\nLet $\\mathrm{G}$ be a group and let $g \\in \\mathrm{G}$. The cyclic subgroup generated by $g$ is the subset:<br>\n$$\\langle g\\rangle=\\left\\{g^{n}: n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\} .$$<br>\nThe following four statements are true for $\\langle g\\rangle$ :<br>\n(i) The cyclic subgroup $&lt;g&gt;$ generated by $g$ is a subgroup of $G$.<br>\n(ii) $g \\in &lt;g&gt;$.<br>\n(iii) If $H \\leq G$ and $g \\in H,$ then $&lt;g&gt; \\leq H.$ Hence $&lt;g&gt;$ is the smallest subgroup of $G$ containing $g.$<br>\n(iv) $&lt;g&gt;$ is abelian.<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"428\" src=\"media/f8/f84b834ed11defaa1ee6.png\" width=\"624\"></strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376073\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{G}$ be a group and let $\\text{H} = \\{x^{–1} | x \\in \\text{G}\\}$ then which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{G} \\subseteq \\text{H}$ but $\\text{G}$ may not be same as $H$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{H} \\subseteq \\text{G}$ but $\\text{H}$ may not be same as $\\text{G}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{H = G}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{H}$ may not be a group.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 20)</small></h4><a name=\"374605\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Since the inverse of an element in $\\text{G}$ is in $\\text{H, H} \\subseteq \\text{G}.$ Let $g$ belong to $\\text{G}.$ Then $g^{–1}$ belongs to $\\text{G}$ and therefore $(g^{–1})^{–1} = g$ belongs to $\\text{G},$ so $\\text{G} \\subseteq \\text{H}.$ So, $\\text{G = H}.$<br>\n<br>\nIn short, every element of $\\text{G}$ is in $\\text{H},$ and there is no extra element in $\\text{H}.$ So, $\\text{H = G}.$</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"413772\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">G is group &nbsp;so G = { e,a , a^2 ,a^3 …..a^-1,a^-2 …..}<br>\n<br>\nH contains inverse of elements present in G so<br>\nH = { e^-1 , a^-1 , a^-2 …………..a^(-1*-1) ,a ^(-2*-1) …..}<br>\nH = { e , &nbsp;&nbsp;a^-1 , &nbsp;a^-2 …………… a ,a^2 ….}<br>\nwe can see that H is same as G<br>\nso H=G</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"498746\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">H contains all inverse of an element x, where element x belong to G. But also note if there is an element x which is inverse of some other y then this x inverse which is y is also in H.<br>\n<br>\n1. Let's check if H is a subset of G<br>\n<br>\nFor every element x in G it' inverse is in G [G is a group] also the inverse say x inverse is also the part of G.<br>\n<br>\nNow, H is the set of all inverses meaning H is a subset of G.<br>\n<br>\n2. Let's check if G is a subset of H <br>\n<br>\nAs said earlier, let a belongs to G now it's inverse ie a inverse is also in G<br>\n<br>\nbut this a inverse must also have a inverse which is a.<br>\n<br>\nand since H is a set of all inverses it will contain both a and a inverse.<br>\n<br>\ntherefore G is a subset of H<br>\n<br>\nand hence both G and H are same ie G = H.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374604\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{G}_{1}$ and $\\text{G}_{2}$ be two groups as following:</p><ol><li>$G_1$ is a group such that $\\forall x, y, z \\in \\text{G}_{1}$</li></ol><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;$xy = zx$ implies $y = z$</p><ol start=\"2\"><li>$\\text{G}_{2}$ is a group such that $\\forall x, y, z \\in \\text{G}_{2}$</li></ol><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $xyz = ayc$ implies $xz = ac$</p><p>where $x, y, z, a, c \\in \\text{G}_{2}$</p><p>Then which of the following is True ?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{G}_{1}$ is abelian, $\\text{G}_{2}$ is not abelian.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Both $\\text{G}_{1}$ and $\\text{G}_{2}$ are abelian.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{G}_{2}$ is abelian, not $\\text{G}_{1}$.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Neither of $\\text{G}_{1}$ and $\\text{G}_{2}$ is abelian.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"374588\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtu.be/27dpP0LS00Q?t=1193\" target=\"_blank\">Find the Detailed Video Solution with many more Variations</a></strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:decimal\" type=\"1\">\n\t<li>For $\\text{G}_{1},$ we know from associativity<br>\n\t$(ab)a = a(ba) \\Rightarrow ab = ba$</li>\n\t<li>For $\\text{G}_{2},$<br>\n\t$b = b$<br>\n\tWe know that &nbsp;<br>\n\t$be = eb$<br>\n\tAnd $e = a a^{-1} = &nbsp;a^{-1} a$ &nbsp;<br>\n\t$a a^{-1} &nbsp;b = b &nbsp;a^{-1} a$<br>\n\tSo, $ba = ab$</li>\n</ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 9)</small></h4><a name=\"385305\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" height=\"955\" src=\"media/25/2532ae1f6d214b976f16.jpg\" width=\"676\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d199bb3e6ca9f05ee9cb.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>This the given answer.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374587\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbf{Z}$ be set of integers, and “$+$” be an integer addition operation.</p><p>Let $p, q$ be distinct primes. If $\\text{J}$ is a proper subgroup of $(\\mathbf{Z}, +)$ containing exactly three of $\\{p, p + q, pq, p^q, q^p\\},$</p><p>(Note $:\\text{J}$ may contain other elements of $\\mathbf{Z}$ which are not in this set), which three elements does $\\text{J}$ include?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\{p, p + q, pq\\}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\{pq, p^{q}, q^{p}\\}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\{p, pq, p^{q}\\}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\{p, pq, q^{p}\\}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 30)</small></h4><a name=\"374586\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>If element $\\text{X}$ belongs to a group then $\\text{X}^{n}$ should also belong to the group due to closure property and here</p>\n\n<p>$\\text{X}^{n} = \\text{X + X + X } +\\dots + \\text{X} = (n\\; \\text{times X})$<br>\n<br>\nThe three elements are $p, pq$, and $p^q$ - these are the ones that can be obtained by repeatedly adding $p$ to itself.<br>\n<br>\nExample : Let $\\text{P} = 3 ; \\text{Q} = 5$<br>\n<br>\nSince $\\text{J}$ is a proper subgroup of $(\\mathbf{Z},+),$ So, J contains identity element i.e. $0;$<br>\n<br>\nIf $\\text{J}$ contains $\\text{P} = 3;$ then $\\text{J}$ also has to contain $\\text{P + P = 2P} = 6 ;$</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$\\text{P + P + P = 3P} = 9 ;$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{P + P + P + P + P = 5P = PQ} = 15$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{P + P} +\\dots + (\\text{P}^{\\text{Q-1}}\\;\\text{times)} = \\text{P}^{\\text{Q}},$ etc.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>$\\textbf{NOTE:}$ If $\\text{J}$ contains both $\\text{P, Q}$ then $\\text{J}$ would also contain $\\text{P+Q} ; \\text{P}^{\\text{Q}} ; \\text{Q}^{\\text{P}} ; \\text{PQ}$ etc..<br>\n<br>\nBUT Since it is given that Exactly $3$ elements from $\\{p, p + q, pq, p^{q}, q^{p}\\};$ Option C is correct.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{NOTE:}$ Since $\\text{J}$ is proper subgroup of $(\\mathbf{Z},+)$ hence, either $\\text{J}$ is infinite set OR $\\text{J} = \\{0\\}.$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"426195\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>There's a clear statement saying that<strong> \"J might contain other elements than that of the elements in the given set\"</strong>&nbsp; ,</p><p>So , try to derive other elements and do not confuse that there will only be three elements in the set J</p><p><strong>Answer: C;</strong></p><p>-&gt;&nbsp;<strong>J has to contain 0, as 0 is identity element for the main group , other than 0 , we have those 3 exact elements from set (at least) , and (at most)&nbsp; we can have many other elements in J.</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"443345\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"627\" height=\"541\" src=\"media/82/821f3ea31fcf2e18e6b7.png\"></p><p>.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"506505\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"25,0,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"25,0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Question:</b> Proper subgroup $J$ containing exactly three of $\\{p, p+q, pq, p^q, q^p\\}$.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"25,1,2\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"25,1,2,0,0\">If $J$ contains $p$, it must contain all multiples of $p$, including $pq$ and $p^q$.<button aria-label=\"View source details. Opens side panel.\" data-hveid=\"0\" decode-data-ved=\"1\" data-ved=\"0CAAQvoAQahgKEwjhgpKK09uRAxUAAAAAHQAAAAAQ7AU\"></button></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"25,1,2,1,0\">It cannot contain $p+q$ because if it contained $p$ and $p+q$, it would have to contain $(p+q) - p = q$. If it contains both primes $p$ and $q$, the subgroup is $1Z$ (all integers), which is not a <i data-path-to-node=\"25,1,2,1,0,2\" data-index-in-node=\"86\">proper</i> subgroup.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"25,1,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"25,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Logic:</b> * Subgroups of $(Z, +)$ are of the form $nZ$ (multiples of $n$).<button aria-label=\"View source details. Opens side panel.\" data-hveid=\"0\" decode-data-ved=\"1\" data-ved=\"0CAAQvoAQahgKEwjhgpKK09uRAxUAAAAAHQAAAAAQ6wU\"></button></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"25,2,1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"25,2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Correct Answer:</b> <b data-path-to-node=\"25,2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"16\">C</b> ($\\{p, pq, p^q\\}$)</p></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"494450\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A useful technique to answer this question is to take any 2 prime numbers P &amp; Q. Then check the options and see if by closure property we are able to get element 1 &amp; since 1 is a generator under addition it will generate whole of Z (including -ve numbers since by inverse property we will also have -1).</p><p><strong>Also Note :&nbsp;</strong>If all 3 elements are ODD or all 3 are EVEN. Then it can never generate 1.<br><br>Check using P = 2 &amp; Q = 5.</p><p>Only option c is all even {2, 10, 32}. It will never generate 1 and hence (J, +) is a proper subgroup of (Z, +).</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374585\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"A binary operation “$\\ast$” on a set $\\text{A}$ is a function from $\\text{A} \\times \\text{A}$ to $\\text{A},$ which maps pair $(a, b)$ to $a \\ast b.$<br>\n<br>\nBinary operation “$\\ast$” on a set $\\text{A}$ is commutative if $a \\ast b = b \\ast a, \\forall a, b \\in \\text{A}.$<br>\n<br>\nThe total number of different commutative binary operations on a set of four elements is?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"374580\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"388\" src=\"media/4d/4d4e4d23e7341f24ac48.png\" width=\"757\"></strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"443847\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>4^10</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6b94c4f50b449b701fd3.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"416055\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c3/c3d46e8c7982f630dd82.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64fa50925a48cf09b863.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p>Please do let me know of any mistakes if occurred.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"408177\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">assume S={a,b,c,d}<br>\n<br>\nfor commutative binary operations we get &nbsp;|s| =<br>\n<br>\n{a*a, a*b , a*c , a*d, b*b, b*c, b*d, c*c, c*d , d*d}<br>\n<br>\n|s| = 10 ..hence total no os comm operation possible = |S| ^ |s| = 4^10</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"500945\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"600\" height=\"776\" src=\"media/32/324cd100ee3fbab0bf4f.jpg\">.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"533402\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><div class=\"arsu_va_iframe-container\"><iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/tWS2SlFHg7U\" class=\"arsu_va_iframe\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe></div>?si=H97ZQ_rk3-PQc-ns &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;deepak sir's explanation..</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374579\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following statements :</p><ol><li>Suppose $\\text{G}$ is a group and $a,x \\in \\text{G}.$ If order of $x=2,$&nbsp;Then $(a x&nbsp;a^{-1})$ has order $2.$</li><li>A finite group is never the union of two of its proper subgroups.</li></ol><p>Which of the above statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Only $1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only $2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Both</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"374578\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Statement 1:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"124\" src=\"media/8f/8fbe4406e589951dca20.png\" width=\"761\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement 2:</strong><br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"140\" src=\"media/13/134b98cd509028c59722.png\" width=\"732\"></strong><br>\n<br>\nthe identity element , we have $|\\text{A} \\cup \\text{B}| \\leq |\\text{G}| - 1.]$<br>\n<br>\nActually, A group (finite or infinite) is never the union of two of its proper subgroups. But proof for infinite groups is not easy and can be skipped.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"436200\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><pre>ii)According to Lagrange's Theorem,if H is a subgroup of G then order of H must be a factor of order of G.\nCase 1:Order of G is odd i.e. |G|=n.\n\nWe have to take maximum possible order to get union order of two subgroups becomes n.\nLet order of two proper subgroup of G be (n-1)/2 and (n+1)/2.\nSince identity element should belong to both subgroups.So no. of remaining elements in both subgroups are (n-3)/2 and (n-1)/2.\nNow for order of union to be n,we have to take all the elements in two subgroups be distinct(except for identity).\nSo order of union of two subgroups becomes (n-3)/2+(n-1)/2+1(1 is for identity)=n-1\nCase 2:Similarly when n=even\nOrder of subgroups be n/2 and n/2\nNumber of elements in both subgroups without identity=n/2-1 ,n/2-1\nSo union =(n/2-1)+(n/2-1)+1=n-1\nTherefore ii)statement is never possible.</pre></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"470424\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Statement-1:</strong></p><p>If order of x = 2, which means x² = e.</p><p>we need to prove (axa⁻¹)² = e</p><p>= a x a⁻¹ a x a⁻¹</p><p>= a x²&nbsp;a ⁻¹</p><p>= a e a ⁻¹ (Given)</p><p>= e (Hence, Proved)</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Statement-2:</strong> (Not a proof, but a simple intuition with an example)</p><p>Any two proper subgroups of G will never be the union = G.</p><p>Because, Let's take a group G of order 14.</p><p>It's proper subgroups order can be (1, 2, 7), Take any two out of it and sum it you will never get the G's order.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"437628\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><blockquote><h3><span style=\"\"><span style=\"\">No group $G$ can be the union of two proper subgroups.</span></span></h3></blockquote><p><strong>Proof:</strong></p><ul><li>Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that \\( G = H \\cup K \\), where \\( H \\) and \\( K \\) are proper subgroups of \\( G \\) (i.e., \\( H \\neq G \\) and \\( K \\neq G \\)).</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Step 1</strong>: \\( H \\not\\subseteq K \\) and \\( K \\not\\subseteq H \\)</p><ul><li>If \\( H \\subseteq K \\), then \\( G = H \\cup K = K \\), which contradicts the assumption that \\( K \\) is a proper subgroup of \\( G \\).</li><li>Similarly, if \\( K \\subseteq H \\), then \\( G = H \\cup K = H \\), which contradicts the assumption that \\( H \\) is a proper subgroup of \\( G \\).</li></ul><p>Thus, neither \\( H \\) is a subset of \\( K \\) nor \\( K \\) is a subset of \\( H \\).</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Existence of elements \\( x \\in G \\setminus H \\) and \\( y \\in G \\setminus K \\)</p><ul><li>Since \\( H \\not\\subseteq K \\), there exists some element \\( x \\in G \\) such that \\( x \\notin H \\). Similarly, since \\( K \\not\\subseteq H \\), there exists some element \\( y \\in G \\) such that \\( y \\notin K \\).</li></ul><div>&nbsp;</div><p>&nbsp;</p><div>&nbsp;</div><p><strong>Step 3</strong>: Consider the product \\( xy \\in G \\)</p><ul><li>Now consider the element \\( xy \\in G \\). Since \\( G = H \\cup K \\), we must have \\( xy \\in H \\) or \\( xy \\in K \\).</li><li><strong>Case 1:</strong> \\( xy \\in H \\)<ul><li>If \\( xy \\in H \\), then since \\( y \\in K \\) (as \\( y \\notin H \\), it must belong to \\( K \\) because \\( G = H \\cup K \\)), we know that \\( y^{-1} \\in K \\) because subgroups are closed under inverses.</li><li>Now, consider the element:\\[(xy) y^{-1} = x.\\]</li><li>Since \\( xy \\in H \\) and \\( y^{-1} \\in K \\), and subgroups are closed under multiplication, \\( (xy) y^{-1} = x \\in H \\). But this contradicts the fact that \\( x \\notin H \\).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Case 2:</strong> \\( xy \\in K \\)<ul><li>If \\( xy \\in K \\), then since \\( x \\in H \\) (as \\( x \\notin K \\), it must belong to \\( H \\) because \\( G = H \\cup K \\)), we know that \\( x^{-1} \\in H \\).</li><li>Now, consider the element:</li><li>\\[ x^{-1}(xy) = y.\\]</li><li>Since \\( xy \\in K \\) and \\( x^{-1} \\in H \\), we have \\( x^{-1}(xy) = y \\in K \\). But this contradicts the fact that \\( y \\notin K \\).</li></ul></li></ul><p>Therefore, a finite group \\( G \\) cannot be the union of two proper subgroups.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"490672\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Proof for statement-2:</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7a6412c82270651dedf6.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533206\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Why a group can't be the union of 2 proper subgroups (An intuitive counting proof)<br>\n<br>\nWe know the textbook proof using $hk \\in H$ or $K$ can feel a bit abstract. Here is a much more intuitive way to think about it using Lagrange's theorem and basic element counting.<br>\nLet's start with a simple example:<br>\n<br>\nTake $G = \\mathbb{Z}_4 = \\{0, 1, 2, 3\\}$. The order of the group is $4$.<br>\n<br>\nBy Lagrange's theorem, any proper subgroup must have an order that divides 4. So the largest possible size for a proper subgroup here is $4/2 = 2$.<br>\n<br>\nLet's pick two subgroups of size 2, say $H = \\{0, 2\\}$ and try to see if we can cover the whole group with another one. If we try to take their union:<br>\n$$|H \\cup K| = |H| + |K| - |H \\cap K|$$<br>\n<br>\nSince every single subgroup must contain the identity element ($0$), they are guaranteed to overlap at least at $\\{0\\}$.<br>\n<br>\nPlugging in the max sizes:<br>\n$$\\text{Max elements in } H \\cup K = 2 + 2 - 1 = 3$$<br>\n<br>\nSince the union can only hold at most 3 elements, it will always leave exactly 1 unique element stranded outside the union (in this case, elements 1 and 3 are left out). So $\\mathbb{Z}_4 \\neq H \\cup K$.<br>\nGeneral Formal Proof:<br>\n<br>\nLet $G$ be a finite group of order $n$, and let $H$ and $K$ be proper subgroups.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Max Subgroup Size: By Lagrange's, since $H$ and $K$ are proper, their maximum possible size is $\\frac{n}{2}$.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Identity Overlap: Since both are valid subgroups, the identity element $e$ must be in both. Therefore, $|H \\cap K| \\ge 1$.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Using Inclusion-Exclusion:<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$$|H \\cup K| = |H| + |K| - |H \\cap K|$$<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Substituting our maximum bounds:<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$$|H \\cup K| \\le \\frac{n}{2} + \\frac{n}{2} - 1$$<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$$|H \\cup K| \\le n - 1$$<br>\n<br>\nSince the maximum number of elements the union can possibly hold is $n - 1$, it is mathematically impossible for $H \\cup K$ to equal the full group $G$ (which has $n$ elements). $\\blacksquare$<br>\n<br>\n(Note: This counting trick is why 3 proper subgroups can successfully mask a group—like in the Klein 4-group—because the extra subgroups give you enough extra unique elements to get past the $n-1$ ceiling constraint).<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374577\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{S}$ be the set of all functions $f : \\text{R} \\rightarrow \\text{R}.$ Consider the two binary operations $+$ and $\\ast$ on $\\text{S}$ defined as pointwise addition and composition of functions, as follows.</p><ul><li>$(f + g)(x) = f (x) + g(x)$</li><li>$(f \\ast g)(x) = f (g(x))$</li></ul><p>Which of the following statements are true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\"><li>$\\ast$ is commutative.</li><li>$+$ and $\\ast$ satisfy the left distributive law i.e. $f \\ast (g + h) = (f \\ast g) + (f \\ast h).$</li><li>$+$ and $\\ast$ satisfy the right distributive law i.e. $(g + h)\\ast f = (g \\ast f)+ (h \\ast f).$</li></ol><p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>All</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>II only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Ill only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>II and III only</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 20)</small></h4><a name=\"445118\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li><pre>(f+g)(x) = f(x) + g(x) ..... (i)</pre></li><li><pre>(f*g)(x) = f(g(x)) ..... (ii)</pre></li></ul><pre><b>1] * is commutative. i.e. we need to prove that, </b><strong>(f*g)(x) = (g*f)(x)</strong>\n\n<b>Proof: </b>L.H.S. = (f*g)(x) = f(g(x)) ; \n\n       R.H.S. = (g*f)(x) = g(f(x)) _using (ii)\n\n       ∴ 1] is <span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\"><strong><em>FALSE</em></strong></span> since L.H.S. ≠ R.H.S. \n\n<b>2] f * (g+h) = (f*g) + (f*h) ; i.e. [f * (g+h)](x) = (f*g)(x) + (f*h)(x)</b>\n\n<b>Proof: </b>L.H.S. = [f*(g+h)](x) = f((g+h)(x)) _using (ii) = f(g(x)+h(x)) _using (i) ; \n\n       R.H.S. = (f*g)(x) + (f*h)(x) = f(g(x)) + f(h(x)) _using (ii)\n\n       ∴ 2] is <span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\"><strong><em>FALSE</em></strong></span> since L.H.S. ≠ R.H.S.\n\n<b>3] (g+h) * f = (g*f) + (h*f) ; i.e. [(g+h) * f](x) = (g*f)(x) + (h*f)(x)</b>\n\n<b>Proof: </b>L.H.S. = [(g+h) * f](x) = (g+h)(f(x)) _using (ii) = g(f(x)) + h(f(x)) _using (i); \n\n       R.H.S. = (g*f)(x) + (h*f)(x) = g(f(x)) + h(f(x)) _using (ii)\n\n       ∴ 3] is <span style=\"color:#27ae60;\"><strong><em>TRUE</em></strong></span> since L.H.S. = R.H.S.</pre></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 19)</small></h4><a name=\"379330\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer: C</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>I) $*$ is commutative:</strong></p>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p>Function composition is associative but not commutative.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>Take for example $f(x) = x^2$ and $g(x) = x + 2$.</p>\n\n<p><strong>LHS</strong>: $fog(x) = f(g(x)) = f(x + 2) = (x + 2)^2$, whereas,</p>\n\n<p><strong>RHS</strong>: $gof(x) = g(x^2) = x^2 + 2$.</p>\n\n<p>$fog(x) \\neq gof(x)$.</p>\n\n<hr>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>II) $+$ and $∗$ satisfy the left distributive law i.e. $f∗(g+h)=(f∗g)+(f∗h)$.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Similar to above proof take some function $h(x) = x/2$.</p>\n\n<p><strong>LHS</strong>: $f * (g + h) = f[g(x) + h(x)] = f(x + 2 + x/2) = (x + 2 + x/2)^2$, whereas,</p>\n\n<p><strong>RHS</strong>: $(f ∗ g) + (f ∗ h) = f(g(x)) + f(h(x)) = f(x + 2) + f(x/2) = (x + 2)^2 + (x/2)^2$.</p>\n\n<p>$f * (g + h) \\neq (f * g) + (f * h)$.</p>\n\n<hr>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>III) $+$ and $∗$ satisfy the right distributive law i.e. $(g + h) ∗ f = (g ∗ f) + (h ∗ f)$. </strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>LHS</strong>: $(g + h) ∗ f = (g + h)(f(x)) = (g + h)(x^2) = x^2 + 2 + x^2/2$.</p>\n\n<p><strong>RHS</strong>: $(g ∗ f) + (h ∗ f) = g(f(x)) + h(f(x)) = g(x^2) + h(x^2) = x^2 + 2 + x^2/2$.</p>\n\n<p>So $(g + h) ∗ f = (g ∗ f) + (h ∗ f)$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"442142\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">I. Consider $\\mathrm{f(x) = -x}\\text{ and g(x) = abs(x) or |x|}$.<br>\n<br>\nThe operation * is commutative iff for every &nbsp;$\\mathrm{f,g} \\in S$, &nbsp;$ \\mathrm{(f* g) =(g * f)}.$<br>\n<br>\nNow $\\mathrm{(f * g)(x) = f(g(x)) = f(|x|) &nbsp;= -|x|} $ and $\\mathrm{(g *f)(x) &nbsp;= g(f(x)) = g(-x) = |-x| = |x|}$<br>\n<br>\njust for an example pick ur $\\mathrm{x = 2}$ then $\\mathrm{(f*g)(x) = -2 }\\text{ and} \\mathrm{(g*f)(x) = 2}$.<br>\nSo clearly option I is false.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nII. By Definition, $\\mathrm{(f*(g+h))(x) = (f((g+h)(x)) = f(g(x) + h(x)) }$<br>\n<br>\nConsider $f(x) = x^2$ and $g(x) = h(x) = x $ then for any $y \\in \\mathrm{R}$, $\\mathrm{(f*(g+h))(y)) = f(y+y) = f(2y) &nbsp;=4y^2}$ and<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;$\\mathrm{((f*g) + (f*h))(y) = (f*g)(y) + (f*h)(y) = f(g(y)) + f(h(y))}$<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$\\mathrm{= f(y) + f(y) = y^2 + y^2 = 2y^2}$.<br>\n<br>\nPick our $y = 1 $, then $\\mathrm{(f*(g+h))(y) = 4 }$ and $\\mathrm{((f*g) + (f*h))(y) = 2}$.<br>\n<br>\nSo clearly Option II is False.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nIII. Suppose u be arbitrary in $\\mathrm{R}$ and let $f , g, h$ be arbitrary in S.<br>\n<br>\nThen By definition $\\mathrm{((g+h)*f)(u) = ((g+h)(f(u)) &nbsp;= g(f(u)) + h(f(u)).}$<br>\n<br>\nSince u is arbitrary in R, we can conclude that the function $\\mathrm{(g+h)*f }$ and $\\mathrm{(g*f) + (h*f)}$ are equal.<br>\n<br>\nHence Option III is correct.<br>\n<br>\nSo Option C is the corret one.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374566\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":25.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":15}],"stats":{"questions":15,"maxMarks":10,"types":{"NAT":4,"MCQ":7,"MSQ":4},"withoutKey":9}};
